00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:03.120
was made possible by Skillshare.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.580
Learn to think like a lawyer
for free for two months

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.322
by clicking on the link
in the description.

00:00:08.322 --> 00:00:09.155
(soft bar piano playing)

00:00:09.155 --> 00:00:11.200
- You know what this means.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.350
They're gonna keep coming after her,

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:16.330
personally, legally, in
whatever way they can.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.310
- Take it to the judge!

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.158
There will be sanctions!

00:00:20.158 --> 00:00:23.325
(light, jaunty music)

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.500
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.680
Today, we are covering
an oldie but a goodie,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.550
Damages episode one, starring
Glenn Close and Rose Byrne.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.040
I've never seen an episode of Damages,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.350
but I've heard a lot about it.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.010
So I'm really looking forward to this.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.820
As always, be sure to comment
in the form of an objection,

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:42.430
which I will either sustain or overrule,

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.680
and let me know what movie
or TV show I should do next.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.360
And, of course, stick around until the end

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.240
of the video where I give this episode

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.360
of Damages a grade for legal realism.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.836
So, without further ado, let's
dig into Damages episode one.

00:00:56.836 --> 00:00:58.510
(jackhammer pounding in distance)

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.510
(car horns honking)

00:01:04.347 --> 00:01:05.983
(taxi honks)
(tires screech)

00:01:05.983 --> 00:01:06.907
(dramatic music)

00:01:06.907 --> 00:01:07.860
Eh, you should really shower up

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.253
after you murdered someone.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.700
The blood is a dead giveaway.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.840
- Ellen, I think you'll find
our offer more than fair

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.400
for someone just out of law school.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.460
We'd like to bring you in
as the junior associate.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.110
Five years guaranteed,

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:25.150
- Okay.
- with a start salary of...

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:27.118
- Holy (bleep).

00:01:27.118 --> 00:01:28.210
(laughs)

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.560
- So it is very common
to get job offers right

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:31.393
out of law school.

00:01:31.393 --> 00:01:33.210
That is one of the primary
functions of law school

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.410
is to connect aspiring lawyers

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.970
with big firms who will
make offers like these.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.130
A couple things to note here.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:41.790
Number one, nobody gets
a five-year guarantee

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:42.720
out of law school.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.400
There's just no way to know who's going

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.610
to be a good lawyer and who's not,

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:47.690
so there's really never such a thing

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:51.310
as a guaranteed employment
contract with a big law firm.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.210
But that kind of reaction
to the salary offer

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.510
is somewhat realistic because
New York really sets the bar

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.600
when it comes to associate salary.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.050
Right now, in New York at a big firm,

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:05.540
you can make anywhere
between 160 and $190,000

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.568
per year straight out of law school

00:02:07.568 --> 00:02:08.401
(cash register chimes)
if you go

00:02:08.401 --> 00:02:09.290
to one of the biggest firms.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:11.720
I don't know if this is
one of those big firms.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.220
It looks very ornate.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.240
But there are some monster salaries

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.483
that are being offered to
students right out of law school.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.473
- Ms. Hughes, the verdict's coming in.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.670
- It's your last chance
to get off cheap, 150.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.300
- 50, Patty, tops.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.863
- Reggie Dwayne Thomas.

00:02:30.829 --> 00:02:32.010
- Who the hell's that?

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.580
- A second grader and a Mets fan.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.480
You know him as patient 61.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.890
As his immune system is being decimated,

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:40.230
Reggie asked me for two things.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.063
- 75, Patty.

00:02:41.063 --> 00:02:43.330
- That his team make the playoffs

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.080
and that someone punish the
company that made him sick.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.600
- All right, (bleep) 150.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.660
That's it, we're done.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.430
- Ah, the good old
courthouse steps negotiation.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.250
While this is clearly a dramatization,

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.490
there are some kernels of truth here

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.520
because, contrary to popular belief,

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.680
very few cases actually go to trial.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.240
And those that do go to trial,

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.410
very few of them actually
end up going to verdict

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.320
because parties are risk averse.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.240
And while tens of millions of dollars

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.460
might seem like a huge
amount, and it is, obviously,

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.900
to a large corporation, the
downside could be even worse.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.940
For example, a good friend
of mine just litigated a case

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.533
against Monsanto and won $300 million.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.720
So if you're up against a 300 million

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.260
or a billion-dollar verdict
that could bankrupt the company,

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:32.000
it's better to just take your lumps,

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.900
get the certainty of not
having hundreds of millions

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.490
of dollars against you by
the jury, and just move on,

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.530
which I think is what's happening here.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.733
- I am here because of Arthur Frobisher.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.480
5,000 of his employees
lost their life savings

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:48.920
as a result of his actions.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.290
- Alleged actions.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:52.780
He was found not guilty
at a criminal trial.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.220
- As is often the case,
there are different burdens

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:56.950
of proof depending on
what court you're in.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:59.510
If you have a criminal prosecution,

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:02.440
then you have the highest
burden in all of law,

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.270
which is you must prove your case

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:07.180
as the state beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.130
The members of the jury

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:11.510
cannot have a single reasonable
doubt in order to convict.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.740
However, what often happens is that,

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.820
even if you are acquitted
in a criminal court,

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:20.170
you can be brought to
trial in a civil court

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.530
where the burden of proof is much lower.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.750
You can get damages.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.310
- Oh boy, I usually only get this excited

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.770
when they say the title
of a movie in the movie.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:31.520
- Even if a defendant is
acquitted in criminal court,

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.340
then you can still sue that person

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:37.450
for civil damages, IE money
damages, in civil court

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.350
where the burden of proof
is much, much lower.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:41.420
You just have to show a
preponderance of the evidence.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:44.320
In other words, if you
had both sides on a scale,

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:47.700
which side is just slightly
more persuasive than the other?

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.130
So I think what's going to happen here

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:52.420
is that there will be a civil
lawsuit against Mr. Frobisher

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.360
for civil damages, money damages,

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.390
where he might have to discourage some

00:04:56.390 --> 00:05:00.120
of the profits that he
made or pay civil damages

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.940
to his employees if he
did something wrong.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:04.480
So I guess we'll see.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.670
- I need you to understand this.

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.970
We are engaging in a battle with a man

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.733
who will stop at nothing
to preserve his fortune.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:17.500
High stakes litigation is
a long and painful process.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.760
- That is true.
- Frobisher will destroy us

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.910
in the press, he will
attack us personally,

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:25.140
he will try to turn our lives upside down

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.590
so that we'll want to settle.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.550
Meanwhile, his attorneys will spin him

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:33.323
as the good guy, the
aggrieved innocent victim.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:39.453
So we have to be patient,
and we have to stay strong.

00:05:40.537 --> 00:05:43.223
But most of all, we have
to all stick together.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:45.753
If we do that,

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:48.630
we'll win.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:49.790
- There is some truth to that.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:52.550
Litigation can take literally years.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:55.540
I've been on cases that took
five years to get to trial

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.550
and took another few years
to deal with the appeals.

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:01.290
Litigation takes a long
time, it takes a huge amount

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.080
of resources, and it's one of the reasons

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:04.580
that people settle all the time,

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:06.680
is because it's so resource intensive

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.020
that it can take years
before you get compensation

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:11.520
when you're clearly an aggrieved party.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.180
It's a real problem.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:14.930
- Ellen, Uncle Pete.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:16.860
Uncle Pete's been with
Patty longer than any of us.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:19.140
He's really the one who
runs everything around here.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:19.973
- [Pete] Good to meet you.

00:06:19.973 --> 00:06:21.650
- Nice to meet you.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:22.710
It's, uh, it's dry cleaning.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:25.553
We've been working on
the Frobisher case 24/7.

00:06:25.553 --> 00:06:29.850
Some of our associates haven't
been able to go home yet.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:30.683
- That's true.

00:06:30.683 --> 00:06:33.160
Big firms actually do
offer dry cleaning services

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.892
to keep their associates
in the office longer.

00:06:35.892 --> 00:06:38.976
(door creaks open)
(dance music in background)

00:06:38.976 --> 00:06:41.340
(door creaks shut)

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.840
- Well, if it isn't the maid of honor.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.420
- What? (laughs)

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:46.460
This is totally insane to suggest

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:47.920
that a partner in a major firm

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:49.550
is going to come down to someone's wedding

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:51.810
to interview them because
they rejected them

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:53.540
for a job interview.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:56.130
They're gonna get hundreds
of rejections a year.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:58.000
That's just the way
that these things work.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.590
There's no such thing as this firm

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:02.310
that everybody has to work
for right out of law school

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:03.460
or while you're in law school.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:05.330
This is this trope that exists in TV

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:07.270
that just doesn't exist in reality.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:10.650
There are hundreds of
great firms out there.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:13.490
There are even lots of
firms that parallel a sort

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:16.250
of Gloria Allred thing
that's going on over here.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:18.850
There's no reason why
any one in particular

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:21.210
would need to work for
one particular attorney.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:22.664
It doesn't work that way.

00:07:22.664 --> 00:07:23.520
(Blondie's "Heart of Glass" playing)

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:24.353
- Ms. Hewes.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.610
Is there any way I could still interview?

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.680
- I think it would be
a waste of time, Ellen.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:36.757
You're hired.

00:07:36.757 --> 00:07:37.860
(scoffs)

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:39.900
- From 1:00 PM to three,
he lunches with a few

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.823
of his Fortune 500 pals,
lobster bisque, Kobe beef.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.900
- But his broker is nowhere to be found.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.680
From 3:00 PM to 6:00 PM, Frobisher's

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.980
at the spa getting a full body treatment.

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:52.350
- For three hours?

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:53.701
What was he getting waxed?

00:07:53.701 --> 00:07:55.580
(zipper unzips)

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:57.300
- 6:00 PM, Frobisher flies home

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.860
in his private jet, arrives at
Teterboro Airport at 9:00 PM.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.000
He's home safe and sound by 10.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.320
- So somehow Frobisher and
his broker communicated

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:08.743
down in Florida, but no
one saw them together.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:10.980
- So this seems like the kind of issue

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:12.800
that would be determined in discovery.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.170
You would get access to all of his emails,

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:16.660
his phone records.

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:18.780
So if there is any communication going on,

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:20.640
they would probably have that information.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.050
They wouldn't need to do any kind

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:25.450
of private investigation
work to track it down,

00:08:25.450 --> 00:08:28.130
which is dramatic for TV
but is not the kind of thing

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:31.030
that actually happens very
much in actual litigation.

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:32.570
I guess we'll see.

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:36.359
- Good news, I was able
to push this through.

00:08:36.359 --> 00:08:39.850
(pleasant piano playing)

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:42.010
- (gasps) My liquor license!

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:44.330
Ah, excellent, thank you.

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:45.680
- Oh God, don't even get me started

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:48.323
about liquor licenses
in New York, it's crazy.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.143
- But Larry,

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:52.573
Patty Hewes.

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:55.470
She's not the answer.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:09:00.393
I mean, she's an egomaniac
and she's gonna lose.

00:09:01.752 --> 00:09:03.220
You know why?

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:04.590
- No, sir.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:05.840
- Because I didn't do it.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:09.420
I'm-I'm a very rich man.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:11.123
I make no apologies for that.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:15.020
But I'm also an innocent
man, and I want to come

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:18.440
to some reasonable solution to this.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:20.910
- If Mr. Frobisher was an attorney here,

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:23.110
this whole meeting would be
completely inappropriate.

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:25.650
If you know that someone
is represented by counsel,

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:27.580
as another attorney you cannot reach

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:30.480
out to the party directly, you
have to go through counsel.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.950
But there is no rule that
prohibits two clients themselves

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:35.250
from talking things out,

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:36.980
even when there's litigation pending.

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:39.750
That being said, it's a
really, really bad idea

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:42.820
because the lawyers are there
to act as intermediaries,

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:45.570
and they are there to hash
things out dispassionately,

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:49.480
whereas when you get the clients
together talking things out

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:50.960
it never goes right.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.770
And I'm guessing that things
are not gonna go right

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.113
in this episode either.

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:59.633
- I think that you and I
together could end this.

00:10:00.952 --> 00:10:04.063
And we'll, and we'll be discreet,
and we'll take care of you

00:10:04.063 --> 00:10:05.845
when this is all settled, but--

00:10:05.845 --> 00:10:07.520
(groans)
all I'm asking for

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:08.353
- Not good.

00:10:08.353 --> 00:10:10.793
- is a number that you think is fair.

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:12.848
- Yeah, that's one of the reasons you

00:10:12.848 --> 00:10:16.180
don't have clients meet
individually amongst each other,

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:18.210
especially here where this guy seems

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:21.240
to be representing a class
or a whole group of people

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.240
who have been wronged by this company.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.830
You can't conspire with a defendant

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.000
and then screw over all the other members

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.310
of the class for your own personal gain.

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:32.850
This is exactly why you
want lawyers in the room,

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.650
to prevent any kind of shenanigans,

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:37.150
because lawyers know
they can get disbarred

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:38.840
for this kind of nonsense.

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:41.390
- Frobisher's net worth is upwards

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:43.357
of two and a half billion dollars.

00:10:43.357 --> 00:10:44.310
(crowd groans)

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:47.840
Now 100 million sounds like
a lot of money, and it is.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:51.644
But it's less than 5% of
what this man is worth.

00:10:51.644 --> 00:10:52.477
(chuckles softly)

00:10:52.477 --> 00:10:54.260
I think we have a strong case.

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.300
- It doesn't matter how
much the defendant is worth.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.130
It matters how much you're going to get

00:10:58.130 --> 00:10:59.800
as a result of the litigation.

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:01.370
I don't know the specifics of this case,

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:04.370
but Glenn Close here may
be doing a big disservice

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:07.260
to all of those class members
because when you're thinking

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:08.840
about a potential recovery,
there are a couple

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:10.650
of things you need to take into account,

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:12.270
not just the top line number.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.170
Number one, no recovery is certain.

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.780
Let's say that there is a 10% chance

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:19.850
that you're going to win
everything that you think you do.

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:21.390
Well, if you think you're entitled

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:24.080
to a billion dollars,
then a 10% chance means

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.760
that's worth about $100 million.

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:28.830
On top of that, there's
the time value of money.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:30.960
Say you get a full recovery,
but it takes you five

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.280
to 10 years to get that.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:33.890
Well, a lot of people would

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:36.120
rather have a smaller amount right now.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:38.540
That's the net present
value of the recovery.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:40.800
And then on top of that, there
are all the attorney's fees

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.700
and court costs that it's going to take

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:45.010
to litigate this thing to the end.

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:46.220
So those are some of the reasons

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:48.860
why you might take a
smaller amount up front

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:50.580
with the certainty of getting it

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:52.540
than to have to deal
with years of litigation

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:54.319
and potentially never
being able to recover

00:11:54.319 --> 00:11:57.570
if the defendant is judgment
proof and goes bankrupt.

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:00.320
So you gotta take those
things into consideration.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:01.750
- I'd like to take it to a jury

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:06.180
and let them decide what Mr.
Arthur Frobisher owes you.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:07.670
- Yeah, I'll bet you would,

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:10.188
but that might not be in their interest.

00:12:10.188 --> 00:12:11.490
(crowd laughs)

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:12.690
- Look at her.

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:13.910
Normally she turns this shit down.

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:15.660
She hates awards.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:16.820
- Then why is she here?

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:17.805
- For the PR.

00:12:17.805 --> 00:12:20.430
- [Patty] The legal system
has just become another tool--

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:22.700
- Both cases, you get
your clients 100 million,

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:23.553
you're a hero.

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:27.290
But if Frobisher gets off that easy,

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:29.505
the whole legal world
will know she tanked it.

00:12:29.505 --> 00:12:32.150
- That is not true at all.

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:34.100
- Tom here is a vegan.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:35.800
- Only for health reasons.

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:40.760
- Health reasons,
(laughs) right. (chuckles)

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:41.593
- That's weird.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:45.520
- I read an interview once
with a Nobel Prize winner,

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:48.340
a physicist genius, married six times.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:50.267
He said, "Don't have kids.

00:12:50.267 --> 00:12:51.987
"Ruins your ambition, keeps you from

00:12:51.987 --> 00:12:54.000
"what you want in life."

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.010
He said to have wives instead.

00:12:57.010 --> 00:12:58.093
You can leave wives.

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:01.130
You can't leave your own kids.

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:03.780
- What is she signing? (laughs)

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:06.580
Why does she need to sign 100
different pieces of paper?

00:13:08.212 --> 00:13:10.295
(laughs)

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:19.120
- You know what I like about you?

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:19.953
- No.

00:13:19.953 --> 00:13:20.790
- There's always something going on.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:22.593
- What's redeemable about her character?

00:13:22.593 --> 00:13:23.940
How would you know that?

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:24.893
She's done nothing!

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:28.710
- And you don't fall for (bleep).

00:13:32.044 --> 00:13:33.787
- Okay, I guess.

00:13:33.787 --> 00:13:35.730
(slow, dramatic music)

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:38.080
She seems like a blank slate
that everyone just projects

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:39.323
what they want onto her.

00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:43.273
- Patty Hewes has a new associate.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:45.610
- So what?

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:47.260
I thought they voted to
accept the settlement.

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:50.226
- Eh, I haven't gotten the call yet.

00:13:50.226 --> 00:13:51.970
And I'm worried.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:54.423
- The new associate's been
talking to Katie Connor.

00:13:54.423 --> 00:13:56.430
- [Frobisher] Who?

00:13:56.430 --> 00:13:57.273
- Our chef.

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:03.456
- Ray, whatever it takes, fix this.

00:14:03.456 --> 00:14:06.495
(slow, dramatic music)

00:14:06.495 --> 00:14:08.253
- Uh oh, witness tampering.

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.150
Don't witness tamper!

00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:12.740
- Saffron?
(eerie music)

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:15.380
- David, if I don't
tell Patty about Katie,

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:17.050
the clients lose the case.

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:18.780
- I'm not sure that you
could be totally objective

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:19.613
about this, Ellen.

00:14:19.613 --> 00:14:20.530
- What is that supposed to mean?

00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:22.430
- That you'll score points with Patty.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:25.926
- I would never do anything to hurt Katie.

00:14:25.926 --> 00:14:27.870
(head thuds against floor)
(intensifying dramatic music)

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:30.241
- Then just keep her out of this!

00:14:30.241 --> 00:14:31.289
(laughs)
(Katie groans in pain)

00:14:31.289 --> 00:14:32.189
- (chuckles) What?

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:36.323
Yep, sorry Saffron.

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:41.580
But how would that even get
her to testify or not testify?

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:43.335
What are they trying to accomplish here?

00:14:43.335 --> 00:14:44.249
(Katie screams)

00:14:44.249 --> 00:14:47.220
(laughs) Quiet, oh my God.

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:49.950
All right, so if you were a lawyer

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:54.100
and you paid someone to kill
a potential witness's dog,

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:56.690
that is certainly a disbarrable offense.

00:14:56.690 --> 00:14:57.810
I really don't even think that that

00:14:57.810 --> 00:14:59.560
would help keep that witness quiet.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:02.950
She doesn't know anything here.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:04.800
What are these people doing?

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:06.940
- You know what this means.

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:09.080
They're gonna keep coming after her,

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:12.080
personally, legally, in
whatever way they can.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.050
- Take it to the judge!

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:16.430
There will be sanctions!

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:19.800
- Ellen,
(soft bar piano playing)

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:21.300
I don't take this for granted.

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:24.803
I know how hard it is for you.

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:27.950
- Well, if Katie can help the case,

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:30.530
then we're doing the right thing.

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:31.363
- Are you?

00:15:31.363 --> 00:15:33.483
- I'll do everything in
my power to protect her.

00:15:34.570 --> 00:15:35.403
- [Ellen] Good.

00:15:36.451 --> 00:15:38.960
- (laughs softly) Lawyers don't
really have that much power

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:39.793
to protect people.

00:15:39.793 --> 00:15:41.583
They're not the police or the FBI.

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:44.540
That's kind of a hollow promise.

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:46.210
If this had happened in one of my cases,

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:48.020
I immediately would have
taken this information

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:48.960
to the judge.

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:51.090
I would've asked for the police or the FBI

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:52.133
to start investigating it.

00:15:52.133 --> 00:15:55.500
This is a serious crime when
you have witness tampered

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:57.530
and you've killed someone's dog.

00:15:57.530 --> 00:15:59.640
Never mess with someone's dog.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:01.540
This is a serious problem
that should result

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:03.420
in sanctions against the defendant,

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:06.580
if not more criminal charges
against the defendant

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:07.790
or his attorneys.

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:09.200
This is not a small thing.

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:10.810
This is clearly something

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:12.370
that deserves its own investigation,

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:14.530
and you shouldn't just
sweep it under the rug

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:17.321
and just continue the litigation
as if nothing has happened.

00:16:17.321 --> 00:16:19.780
(dramatic music)

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:22.380
- Maybe one of these days
I'll stop being impressed.

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:26.150
Ellen figures out why you hired her,

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:29.061
and you still get her
to deliver the witness.

00:16:29.061 --> 00:16:31.653
- (scoffs) You don't need
to deliver a witness.

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:35.740
- Are you interviewing
for a new associate?

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:38.033
- No, I like Ellen.

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:41.080
I think she's gonna
have a brilliant future.

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:42.133
- Oh, come on.

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:45.920
Okay, so they killed the dog

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:49.530
to force Ellen's friend to testify.

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:51.870
But if they knew anything
about litigation,

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:54.040
all they would have to
do is to issue a subpoena

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:56.350
against the friend and
then depose the friend,

00:16:56.350 --> 00:16:58.630
regardless of whether
she wanted to or not.

00:16:58.630 --> 00:17:02.430
It's so stupid to try
and reverse blackmail

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:06.020
or reverse witness tamper... (sighs)

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:07.350
It doesn't even sound like she was really

00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:08.910
on the fence in the first place.

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:11.530
There are mechanisms to
do this in litigation.

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:13.010
You deal with witnesses who don't want

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:14.633
to testify all the time.

00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:18.240
Ugh! (laughs)

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:21.358
These people are terrible
lawyers, terrible lawyers.

00:17:21.358 --> 00:17:23.588
(dramatic music)

00:17:23.588 --> 00:17:25.290
(dog tags jingle)

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:29.858
Yeah, souvenir for the dog
you murdered for no reason.

00:17:29.858 --> 00:17:33.025
(light, jaunty music)

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:38.260
Okay, so now it's time
to give the first episode

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:41.438
of Damages a grade for legal realism.

00:17:41.438 --> 00:17:43.730
(gavel bangs three times)

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:45.150
Oh boy, it started out strong,

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:47.570
but it really took a
nosedive there at the end.

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:49.670
It started out with some
really good negotiations,

00:17:49.670 --> 00:17:52.230
some interesting interplay
between defendants

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.430
and a class of representatives,

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:57.240
realistic negotiations and back and forth.

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:59.570
But then it took a nosedive
and turned into a soap opera

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:02.280
with witness tampering for no reason,

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:04.300
ignoring the rules of civil procedure.

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:06.010
They could've gotten everything
that they wanted just

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:08.450
through normal channels in litigation,

00:18:08.450 --> 00:18:11.440
but they don't really
seem to be great lawyers.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:14.070
So, all in all, I'm
giving the first episode

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:16.650
of Damages a C plus.

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:20.020
Patty Hewes and Ellen Parsons
failed at being good lawyers.

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:21.330
Learning to think like a lawyer

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:23.580
really means learning
a broad range of topics

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:25.690
and always learning new skills.

00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:27.060
For example, I always wanted to know

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.060
how to buy good wine
that I'll actually like,

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:30.960
so I started taking this
course on wine tasting

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:33.020
by Gary Vaynerchuk on Skillshare.

00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:35.094
I hate having to guess my
way through a wine list

00:18:35.094 --> 00:18:37.970
when, really, all I want is a
good wine to go with dinner.

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:39.510
So I'm finally learning the fundamentals

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:42.170
of tasting, selecting,
and shopping for wine.

